1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:06,462
Chapter 13: Gestational Trophoblastic Disease.

3
00:00:06,462 --> 00:00:27,937
Hydatidiform Mole—Molar Pregnancy Pathogenesis Diagnosis Management Gestational Trophoblastic Neoplasia Diagnosis, Staging, And Prognostic Scoring Treatment Subsequent Pregnancy References Gestational trophoblastic disease (GTD) is the term used to encompass a group of tumors typified by abnormal trophoblast proliferation.

4
00:00:27,937 --> 00:00:32,875
Trophoblast produces human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG).

5
00:00:32,875 --> 00:00:41,062
Thus, the measurement of this peptide hormone in serum is essential for GTD diagnosis, management, and surveillance.

6
00:00:41,062 --> 00:00:52,687
GTD histologically is divided into hydatidiform moles, which are characterized by the presence of villi, and nonmolar trophoblastic malignant neoplasms, which lack villi.

7
00:00:52,687 --> 00:00:57,450
Hydatidiform moles are excessively edematous immature placentas .

8
00:00:57,450 --> 00:01:03,012
These include the benign complete hydatidiform mole and partial hydatidiform mole .

9
00:01:03,012 --> 00:01:06,412
The third member is the malignant invasive mole .

10
00:01:06,412 --> 00:01:14,800
Invasive mole is deemed malignant because of its marked penetration into and destruction of the myometrium and its ability to metastasize.

11
00:01:14,800 --> 00:01:17,300
Table 13-1.

12
00:01:17,300 --> 00:01:29,350
Modified WHO Classification of GTD Nonmolar trophoblastic neoplasms include choriocarcinoma, placental site trophoblastic tumor, and epithelioid trophoblastic tumor .

13
00:01:29,350 --> 00:01:34,050
These three are differentiated by the trophoblast type that they contain.

14
00:01:34,050 --> 00:01:42,512
Under the GTD umbrella term, the malignant forms of GTD are termed gestational trophoblastic neoplasia (GTN).

15
00:01:42,512 --> 00:01:51,150
These include invasive mole, choriocarcinoma, placental site trophoblastic tumor, and epithelioid trophoblastic tumor.

16
00:01:51,150 --> 00:01:59,012
These malignancies develop weeks or years following any type of pregnancy, but more frequently follow hydatidiform mole.

17
00:01:59,012 --> 00:02:06,500
Each GTN malignancy type is histologically distinct and varies in its propensity to invade and metastasize.

18
00:02:06,500 --> 00:02:13,037
However, these diagnoses are infrequently identified from an actual histological specimen.

19
00:02:13,037 --> 00:02:21,500
Instead, measurement of serum hCG levels and clinical findings are more often used to diagnose and treat this malignancy.

20
00:02:21,500 --> 00:02:27,225
Accordingly, GTN is often managed as a single composite clinical entity.

21
00:02:27,225 --> 00:02:31,687
With chemotherapy, most tumors currently are highly curable.

22
00:02:31,687 --> 00:02:33,812
Hydatidiform Mole.

23
00:02:33,812 --> 00:02:41,312
Classic histological findings of molar pregnancy include trophoblast proliferation and villi with stromal edema .

24
00:02:41,312 --> 00:02:54,137
The degree of histological changes, karyotype and immunostaining differences, and the absence or presence of embryonic elements are used to classify them as either complete or partial hydatidiform moles .

25
00:02:54,137 --> 00:03:01,850
These two also vary in their associated risks for developing medical comorbidities and postevacuation GTN.

26
00:03:01,850 --> 00:03:07,150
Of the two, GTN more frequently follows complete hydatidiform mole.

27
00:03:07,150 --> 00:03:09,675
Figure 13-1.

28
00:03:09,675 --> 00:03:12,262
Complete hydatidiform mole.

29
00:03:12,262 --> 00:03:16,937
A. Gross specimen with characteristic vesicles of variable size.

30
00:03:16,937 --> 00:03:20,712
Photograph contributed by Dr. Sasha Andrews.

31
00:03:20,712 --> 00:03:30,675
Reproduced with permission from Patel S, Roberts S, Rogers V, et al [eds]: Williams Obstetrics Study Guide, 25th ed.

32
00:03:30,675 --> 00:03:35,062
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2019.

33
00:03:35,062 --> 00:03:43,912
B. Low-magnification photomicrograph shows generalized edema and cistern formation (black asterisks) within avascular villi.

34
00:03:43,912 --> 00:03:49,500
Haphazard trophoblastic hyperplasia is marked by a yellow asterisk on the right.

35
00:03:49,500 --> 00:03:53,250
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Erika Fong.

36
00:03:53,250 --> 00:03:55,775
Table 13-2.

37
00:03:55,775 --> 00:04:04,975
Features of Partial and Complete Hydatidiform Moles Grossly, complete moles have abnormal chorionic villi that appear as a mass of clear vesicles.

38
00:04:04,975 --> 00:04:09,737
These vary in size and often hang in clusters from thin pedicles.

39
00:04:09,737 --> 00:04:17,787
In contrast, partial molar pregnancies have focal and less advanced hydatidiform changes and contain some fetal tissue.

40
00:04:17,787 --> 00:04:27,512
Both forms of moles usually fill the uterine cavity, but they rarely may be ectopic (Sebire, 2005; Yamada, 2016).

41
00:04:27,512 --> 00:04:31,050
# Epidemiology and Risk Factors.

42
00:04:31,050 --> 00:04:43,312
Ethnic predisposition is seen with hydatidiform mole, and prevalences are higher in Asians, Hispanics, and American Indians (Choi, 2015; Drake, 2006).

43
00:04:43,312 --> 00:04:54,212
The incidence in the United States and Europe has been relatively constant at 1 per 1000 deliveries (Eysbouts, 2016; Melamed, 2016).

44
00:04:54,212 --> 00:04:59,050
The strongest risk factors are age and a prior hydatidiform mole.

45
00:04:59,050 --> 00:05:02,500
Women at both extremes of reproductive age

46
00:05:02,668 --> 00:05:08,893
are most vulnerable (Savage, 2013; Sebire, 2002a).

47
00:05:08,843 --> 00:05:18,180
With a prior complete mole, the risk of another mole is 0.9 percent, and with a previous partial mole, the rate is 0.3 percent.

48
00:05:18,180 --> 00:05:24,068
After two prior complete moles, approximately 20 percent of women have a third mole .

49
00:05:24,068 --> 00:05:26,618
# Pathogenesis.

50
00:05:26,618 --> 00:05:32,018
Molar pregnancies typically arise from chromosomally abnormal fertilizations.

51
00:05:32,018 --> 00:05:36,630
Complete moles most often have a diploid chromosomal composition.

52
00:05:36,630 --> 00:05:44,243
These usually are 46,XX and result from androgenesis, meaning both sets of chromosomes are paternal.

53
00:05:44,243 --> 00:05:48,493
The chromosomes of the ovum are either absent or inactivated.

54
00:05:48,493 --> 00:05:58,005
As shown in Figure 13-2A, an ovum is fertilized by a haploid sperm, which then duplicates its own chromosomes after meiosis.

55
00:05:58,005 --> 00:06:13,743
Less commonly, the chromosomal pattern may be 46,XY or 46,XX and due to fertilization by two sperm, that is, dispermic fertilization or dispermy (Lawler, 1991; Ohama, 1981).

56
00:06:13,743 --> 00:06:16,243
Figure 13-2.

57
00:06:16,243 --> 00:06:20,105
Typical pathogenesis of complete and partial moles.

58
00:06:20,105 --> 00:06:31,668
A. A 46,XX complete mole may be formed if a 23,X-bearing haploid sperm penetrates a 23,X-containing haploid egg whose genes have been “inactivated.

59
00:06:31,668 --> 00:06:39,030
Paternal chromosomes then duplicate to create a 46,XX diploid complement solely of paternal origin.

60
00:06:39,030 --> 00:06:51,905
B. A partial mole may be formed if two sperm—either 23,X- or 23,Y-bearing—both fertilize (dispermy) a 23,X-containing haploid egg whose genes have not been inactivated.

61
00:06:51,905 --> 00:06:58,780
The resulting fertilized egg is triploid with two chromosome sets being donated by the father (diandry).

62
00:06:58,780 --> 00:07:09,155
Partial moles have a triploid karyotype, which is 69,XXX, 69,XXY, or much less commonly, 69,XYY.

63
00:07:09,155 --> 00:07:19,068
These are each composed of two paternal haploid sets of chromosomes contributed by dispermy and one maternal haploid set (see Fig. 13-2B).

64
00:07:19,068 --> 00:07:26,805
Less frequently, a similar haploid egg may be fertilized by an unreduced diploid 46,XY sperm.

65
00:07:26,805 --> 00:07:38,293
These triploid zygotes result in some embryonic development, however, it ultimately is a lethal fetal condition (Joergensen, 2014; Lakovschek, 2011).

66
00:07:38,293 --> 00:07:45,430
Fetuses that reach advanced ages have severe growth restriction, multiple congenital anomalies, or both.

67
00:07:45,430 --> 00:07:54,155
Twin Pregnancy Rarely, in some twin pregnancies, one chromosomally normal fetus is paired with a complete diploid molar pregnancy.

68
00:07:54,155 --> 00:08:02,030
Importantly, these cases must be distinguished from a single partial molar pregnancy with its associated abnormal fetus.

69
00:08:02,030 --> 00:08:10,018
Other potential diagnoses include placental mesenchymal dysplasia, subchorionic hematoma, or chorioangioma .

70
00:08:10,018 --> 00:08:20,168
To help distinguish among these, chorionic villus sampling, amniocentesis, or fetal cord blood sampling coupled with fetal karyotyping aid confirmation .

71
00:08:20,168 --> 00:08:24,955
Several unique pregnancy problems complicate such twin pregnancies.

72
00:08:24,955 --> 00:08:30,693
Thyrotoxicosis is common, but the most worrisome are preeclampsia or hemorrhage.

73
00:08:30,693 --> 00:08:34,343
These frequently necessitate preterm delivery.

74
00:08:34,343 --> 00:08:39,730
Thus, many women may choose to terminate the gestation, if diagnosed early.

75
00:08:39,730 --> 00:08:48,480
In those with continuing pregnancy, survival of the normal fetus varies and depends on associated comorbidity from the molar component.

76
00:08:48,480 --> 00:08:56,705
Wee and Jauniaux (2005) reviewed outcomes in 174 women, of whom 82 chose termination.

77
00:08:56,705 --> 00:09:08,155
Of the remaining 92 pregnancies, 42 percent either miscarried or had a perinatal death; approximately 60 percent delivered preterm; and 40 percent delivered at term.

78
00:09:08,155 --> 00:09:14,418
Another concern for those continuing their pregnancy is the risk for developing subsequent GTN.

79
00:09:14,418 --> 00:09:24,943
Most evidence indicates no significant difference between women who continue or terminate their pregnancy (Lin, 2017; Sebire 2002b).

80
00:09:24,943 --> 00:09:27,580
# Clinical Findings.

81
00:09:27,580 --> 00:09:35,880
Molar pregnancy is diagnosed sooner than in the past because prenatal care is sought much earlier and sonography is virtually universal.

82
00:09:35,880 --> 00:09:48,655
For example, in 194 women with a complete mole, evacuation was completed at a median gestational age of 9 weeks and at 12 weeks for 172 patients with a partial mole .

83
00:09:48,655 --> 00:09:54,118
As a result, most molar pregnancies are treated before complications ensue.

84
00:09:54,118 --> 00:09:58,693
Typically, 1 to 2 months of amenorrhea precede the diagnosis.

85
00:09:58,693 --> 00:10:05,168
As gestation advances, symptoms tend to be more pronounced with complete compared with partial moles .

86
00:10:05,168 --> 00:10:09,080
Untreated molar pregnancies will almost always cause

87
00:10:09,244 --> 00:10:13,394
uterine bleeding that varies from spotting to profuse hemorrhage.

88
00:10:13,344 --> 00:10:20,856
Bleeding may presage spontaneous molar abortion, but more often, it follows an intermittent course for weeks to months.

89
00:10:20,856 --> 00:10:28,094
In more advanced moles with considerable concealed uterine hemorrhage, moderate iron-deficiency anemia develops.

90
00:10:28,094 --> 00:10:31,619
Nausea and vomiting also may be significant.

91
00:10:31,619 --> 00:10:39,806
Of physical findings, many women have uterine growth exceeding that expected, and the enlarged uterus is comparatively softer.

92
00:10:39,806 --> 00:10:43,431
Fetal heart motion is absent with complete moles.

93
00:10:43,431 --> 00:10:48,256
The ovaries can be fuller and cystic from multiple theca-lutein cysts .

94
00:10:48,256 --> 00:10:55,844
These are more common with a complete mole and likely result from ovarian overstimulation by excessive hCG levels.

95
00:10:55,844 --> 00:11:04,494
Remember that hCG and luteinizing hormone share the same receptor, and both can stimulate the theca layer that surrounds follicles.

96
00:11:04,494 --> 00:11:13,806
Because theca-lutein cysts regress following pregnancy evacuation and subsequent hCG level decline, expectant management is preferred.

97
00:11:13,806 --> 00:11:19,356
Occasionally, a larger cyst may undergo torsion, infarction, and hemorrhage.

98
00:11:19,356 --> 00:11:25,831
However, oophorectomy is not performed unless extensive infarction persists after untwisting.

99
00:11:25,831 --> 00:11:28,381
Figure 13-3.

100
00:11:28,381 --> 00:11:34,894
Sonogram of an ovary with multiple theca-lutein cysts in a woman with a complete hydatidiform mole.

101
00:11:34,894 --> 00:11:47,481
The thyrotropin-like effects of hCG frequently cause serum free thyroxine (fT4) levels to be elevated and thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) levels to be decreased .

102
00:11:47,481 --> 00:11:56,819
Despite this, clinically apparent thyrotoxicosis is unusual and in our experience can be mimicked by bleeding and sepsis from infected products.

103
00:11:56,819 --> 00:12:03,156
Moreover, the serum free T4 levels rapidly normalize after uterine evacuation.

104
00:12:03,156 --> 00:12:07,806
Despite this, cases of presumed “thyroid storm” have been reported .

105
00:12:07,806 --> 00:12:13,894
Severe preeclampsia and eclampsia are relatively common with advanced molar pregnancies.

106
00:12:13,894 --> 00:12:19,731
However, these are seldom seen today because of early diagnosis and evacuation.

107
00:12:19,731 --> 00:12:25,794
An exception is the case of a normal fetus coexisting with a complete mole, described earlier.

108
00:12:25,794 --> 00:12:32,256
In continuing twin gestations, severe preeclampsia frequently mandates preterm delivery.

109
00:12:32,256 --> 00:12:34,656
# Diagnosis.

110
00:12:34,656 --> 00:12:43,319
Serum β-HCG Measurements Most women initially have irregular bleeding that almost always prompts pregnancy testing and sonography.

111
00:12:43,319 --> 00:12:47,619
Less often, women will spontaneously pass molar tissue.

112
00:12:47,619 --> 00:12:55,856
With a complete molar pregnancy, serum β-hCG levels are commonly elevated above those expected for gestational age.

113
00:12:55,856 --> 00:13:00,519
With more advanced moles, values in the millions are not unusual.

114
00:13:00,519 --> 00:13:07,006
Importantly, these high values can lead to erroneous false-negative urine pregnancy test results.

115
00:13:07,006 --> 00:13:15,869
Termed a hook effect, excessive β-hCG hormone levels oversaturate the assay’s targeting antibody and create a false-negative reading .

116
00:13:15,869 --> 00:13:23,506
In these cases, serum β-hCG determinations with or without sample dilution will yield a positive result.

117
00:13:23,506 --> 00:13:33,906
With a partial mole, β-hCG levels may also be significantly elevated, but more commonly concentrations are in ranges expected for gestational age.

118
00:13:33,906 --> 00:13:41,494
Sonography Although this is the mainstay of trophoblastic disease diagnosis, not all cases are confirmed initially.

119
00:13:41,494 --> 00:13:51,931
In a large series of more than 1000 patients with molar pregnancy, sonography’s sensitivity was 44 percent, and its specificity was 74 percent .

120
00:13:51,931 --> 00:14:01,644
With grayscale sonography, a complete mole appears as an echogenic uterine mass filling the endometrial cavity and is surrounded by normal myometrium.

121
00:14:01,644 --> 00:14:09,894
The mass is composed of numerous anechoic cystic spaces of different sizes and shapes, but without a fetus or amnionic sac.

122
00:14:09,894 --> 00:14:13,394
The appearance is often described as a “snowstorm” .

123
00:14:13,394 --> 00:14:19,081
Application of color Doppler displays marked surrounding myometrial vascularity.

124
00:14:19,081 --> 00:14:24,731
However, absent internal flow reflects the avascular villi of complete moles.

125
00:14:24,731 --> 00:14:27,306
Figure 13-4.

126
00:14:27,306 --> 00:14:30,394
Sonograms of hydatidiform moles.

127
00:14:30,394 --> 00:14:35,031
A. Transverse view of a uterus with a complete hydatidiform mole.

128
00:14:35,031 --> 00:14:44,256
The characteristic “snowstorm” appearance reflects an echogenic uterine mass, marked by calipers, that has numerous anechoic cystic spaces.

129
00:14:44,256 --> 00:14:48,269
Notably, a fetus or amnionic sac is absent.

130
00:14:48,269 --> 00:14:54,781
B. In this sagittal image of a partial hydatidiform mole, the fetal head (arrow) lies

131
00:14:54,940 --> 00:14:58,902
adjacent to an enlarged, multicystic placenta.

132
00:14:58,852 --> 00:15:05,402
A partial mole has features that include a thick, multicystic placenta plus a fetus or fetal tissue.

133
00:15:05,402 --> 00:15:09,227
Thin septa can be found within the gestational sac .

134
00:15:09,227 --> 00:15:13,152
Affected fetuses usually die in the first trimester.

135
00:15:13,152 --> 00:15:20,452
Those advancing further often show growth restriction, oligohydramnios, and limb or CNS defects .

136
00:15:20,452 --> 00:15:28,077
In early pregnancy, however, these sonographic characteristics are seen in fewer than half of hydatidiform moles.

137
00:15:28,077 --> 00:15:37,502
At earlier gestations, a complete mole may appear as a polypoid hyperechoic mass that lacks internal cysts and is surrounded by anechoic fluid .

138
00:15:37,502 --> 00:15:41,402
The most common mimics are incomplete or missed abortion.

139
00:15:41,402 --> 00:15:48,065
In these cases, histological evaluation, described next, ultimately is diagnostic.

140
00:15:48,065 --> 00:15:56,277
Occasionally, molar pregnancy may be confused for a multifetal pregnancy or a uterine leiomyoma with cystic degeneration.

141
00:15:56,277 --> 00:16:04,402
Pathology Because of the risk for subsequent GTN following molar pregnancy, postevacuation surveillance is indicated.

142
00:16:04,402 --> 00:16:12,102
Thus, moles must be distinguished from other pregnancy types that are not molar but that have hydropic placental degeneration.

143
00:16:12,102 --> 00:16:21,465
Most often, these are hydropic abortuses formed by the traditional union of one haploid egg and one haploid sperm but are pregnancies that have failed.

144
00:16:21,465 --> 00:16:31,127
Their placentas display hydropic degeneration, in which villi are edematous and swollen, and thus mimic some villous features of hydatidiform moles.

145
00:16:31,127 --> 00:16:35,527
These mimics do not require postevacuation surveillance.

146
00:16:35,527 --> 00:16:41,240
Some distinguishing histological characteristics are shown in Table 13-2.

147
00:16:41,240 --> 00:16:47,515
In pregnancies before 10 weeks, classic molar histological changes may not be apparent.

148
00:16:47,515 --> 00:16:53,477
Villi may not be enlarged, and molar stroma may not yet be edematous and avascular.

149
00:16:53,477 --> 00:17:02,477
Histopathologic evaluation can be enhanced by immunohistochemical staining for p57 expression and by molecular genotyping .

150
00:17:02,477 --> 00:17:09,765
p57KIP2 is a nuclear protein whose gene is paternally imprinted and maternally expressed.

151
00:17:09,765 --> 00:17:15,265
This means that the gene product is produced only in tissues containing a maternal allele.

152
00:17:15,265 --> 00:17:25,865
Because complete moles contain only paternal genes, the p57KIP2 protein is absent in complete moles, and tissues do not pick up this stain .

153
00:17:25,865 --> 00:17:37,390
In contrast, this nuclear protein is strongly expressed in normal placentas, in spontaneous pregnancy losses with hydropic degeneration, and in partial hydatidiform moles .

154
00:17:37,390 --> 00:17:46,015
Accordingly, immunostaining for p57KIP2 is an effective means to isolate complete mole from the diagnostic list.

155
00:17:46,015 --> 00:17:56,402
For distinction of a partial mole from a nonmolar hydropic abortus, both of which express p57KIP2, molecular genotyping can be used .

156
00:17:56,402 --> 00:18:00,977
Molecular genotyping determines the parental source of alleles.

157
00:18:00,977 --> 00:18:12,965
Thereby, it can distinguish among a diploid diandric genome (complete mole), a triploid diandric-monogynic genome (partial mole), or biparental diploidy (nonmolar abortus) .

158
00:18:12,965 --> 00:18:15,190
# Management.

159
00:18:15,190 --> 00:18:25,315
Maternal deaths from molar pregnancies are rare because of early diagnosis, timely evacuation, and vigilant postevacuation surveillance for GTN .

160
00:18:25,315 --> 00:18:38,077
Preoperative evaluation strives to identify potential complications, such as preeclampsia, hyperthyroidism, anemia, electrolyte depletions from hyperemesis, and metastatic disease .

161
00:18:38,077 --> 00:18:52,815
Most recommend chest radiography, whereas computed tomography (CT) and magnetic resonance (MR) imaging are not routinely done unless a chest radiograph shows lung lesions or unless other extrauterine disease is suspected.

162
00:18:52,815 --> 00:18:55,352
Table 13-3.

163
00:18:55,352 --> 00:19:07,202
Some Considerations for Management of Hydatidiform Mole Molar Pregnancy Termination Regardless of uterine size, molar evacuation by suction curettage usually is the preferred treatment.

164
00:19:07,202 --> 00:19:13,927
Preoperative cervical dilation with an osmotic dilator is recommended if the cervix is minimally dilated.

165
00:19:13,927 --> 00:19:21,365
Intraoperative bleeding can be greater with molar pregnancy than with a comparably sized uterus containing nonmolar products.

166
00:19:21,365 --> 00:19:29,452
Thus with large moles, adequate anesthesia, sufficient intravenous access, and blood-banking support is imperative.

167
00:19:29,452 --> 00:19:36,227
A step by step description of dilation and curettage is found in Chapter 11 (p. 213).

168
00:19:36,227 --> 00:19:41,515
For molar evacuation, the cervix is mechanically dilated to preferably

169
00:19:41,668 --> 00:19:45,555
allow insertion of a large Karman suction cannula.

170
00:19:45,505 --> 00:19:51,380
Depending on uterine size, a 10- to 14-mm diameter is typical.

171
00:19:51,380 --> 00:19:56,468
As evacuation is begun, oxytocin is infused to limit bleeding.

172
00:19:56,468 --> 00:20:04,630
Intraoperative sonography is often recommended to help ensure complete uterine cavity emptying and minimize perforation risk.

173
00:20:04,630 --> 00:20:12,505
When the myometrium has contracted, a thorough but gentle curettage with a sharp large-loop Sims curette may be performed.

174
00:20:12,505 --> 00:20:20,068
If bleeding continues despite uterine evacuation and oxytocin infusion, other uterotonic agents are given .

175
00:20:20,068 --> 00:20:27,293
Uncommonly, pelvic arterial embolization, uterine packing, or hysterectomy may be necessary .

176
00:20:27,293 --> 00:20:33,305
Some volume of trophoblast is deported into the pelvic venous system during molar evacuation .

177
00:20:33,305 --> 00:20:43,068
With large moles, the amount of tissue may be sufficient to produce clinically apparent respiratory insufficiency, pulmonary edema, or even embolism .

178
00:20:43,068 --> 00:20:52,143
In our earlier experiences with substantial moles, these and their chest radiographic manifestations clear rapidly without specific treatment.

179
00:20:52,143 --> 00:21:03,043
Following curettage, anti-D immunoglobulin (Rhogam) is given to Rh Dnegative women because fetal tissues with a partial mole may include red cells with D-antigen .

180
00:21:03,043 --> 00:21:14,855
Those with suspected complete mole are similarly treated because a definitive diagnosis of complete versus partial mole may not be confirmed until histological evaluation of the evacuated products.

181
00:21:14,855 --> 00:21:23,493
Following evacuation, the long-term prognosis for women with a hydatidiform mole is not improved with prophylactic chemotherapy.

182
00:21:23,493 --> 00:21:33,880
Moreover, chemotherapy toxicity can be significant, and thus it is not recommended routinely (Gueye, 2014; Wang, 2017).

183
00:21:33,880 --> 00:21:38,843
Methods other than suction curettage can be considered for select cases.

184
00:21:38,843 --> 00:21:47,518
Hysterectomy with ovarian preservation may be preferable for women who have finished childbearing and who carry complete moles with high-risk features.

185
00:21:47,518 --> 00:22:00,930
Of women aged 40 to 49 years, up to 50 percent will subsequently develop GTN, and hysterectomy markedly reduces this likelihood (Elias, 2012; Zhao, 2019).

186
00:22:00,930 --> 00:22:09,305
During hysterectomy, theca-lutein cysts do not require removal, and they spontaneously regress following molar termination.

187
00:22:09,305 --> 00:22:16,168
Last, labor induction or hysterotomy is seldom used for molar evacuation in the United States.

188
00:22:16,168 --> 00:22:23,193
Both will likely increase blood loss and theoretically may raise the incidence of persistent trophoblastic disease .

189
00:22:23,193 --> 00:22:32,318
Postevacuation Surveillance Close biochemical surveillance for persistent gestational neoplasia follows each hydatidiform mole evacuation.

190
00:22:32,318 --> 00:22:40,105
Serial measurement of serum β-hCG levels aim to detect persistent or renewed trophoblastic proliferation.

191
00:22:40,105 --> 00:22:47,205
As a glycoprotein, hCG shows structural heterogeneity and exists in different isoforms.

192
00:22:47,205 --> 00:22:57,368
Thus, for surveillance, an hCG assay that can detect all forms of hCG should be used (Harvey, 2010; Ngan, 2018).

193
00:22:57,368 --> 00:23:03,568
These are different from those used for routine pregnancy testing (de Medeiros, 2009).

194
00:23:03,568 --> 00:23:09,368
The initial βhCG level is obtained within 48 hours after evacuation.

195
00:23:09,368 --> 00:23:16,918
This serves as the baseline, which is compared with β-hCG quantification done thereafter every 1 to 2 weeks.

196
00:23:16,918 --> 00:23:20,343
Levels are followed until they become undetectable.

197
00:23:20,343 --> 00:23:27,080
The median time for such resolution is 6 weeks for partial moles and 7 weeks for complete moles.

198
00:23:27,080 --> 00:23:36,668
Of patients, 95 percent have normal β-hCG levels by 14 weeks postevacuation and 99 percent by 25 weeks .

199
00:23:36,668 --> 00:23:47,655
Once β-hCG is undetectable, this is confirmed with monthly determinations for another 6 months (Lurain, 2010; Sebire, 2007).

200
00:23:47,655 --> 00:23:56,305
Concurrently, reliable contraception is imperative to avoid confusion caused by rising β-hCG levels from a new pregnancy.

201
00:23:56,305 --> 00:24:04,905
Most recommend combination hormonal contraception, injectable depot medroxyprogesterone acetate, or progestin implant.

202
00:24:04,905 --> 00:24:10,393
The latter two are particularly useful if poor patient compliance is anticipated.

203
00:24:10,393 --> 00:24:19,905
Intrauterine devices are not used until βhCG levels are undetectable because of the risk of uterine perforation if an invasive mole is present.

204
00:24:19,905 --> 00:24:25,155
After these 6 months, monitoring is discontinued and pregnancy allowed.

205
00:24:25,155 --> 00:24:32,368
Although not recommended, if a woman conceives during surveillance, live-birth and congenital anomalies rates

206
00:24:32,524 --> 00:24:35,799
appear to mirror general population rates .

207
00:24:35,749 --> 00:24:43,886
During β-hCG levels surveillance, increasing or persistently plateaued levels mandate evaluation for GTN.

208
00:24:43,886 --> 00:24:51,199
If the woman has not become pregnant, these levels signify trophoblast proliferation that is most likely malignant.

209
00:24:51,199 --> 00:24:56,661
Several factors predispose a patient to GTN following molar evacuation.

210
00:24:56,661 --> 00:25:05,036
Complete moles have a 15 to 20 percent incidence of malignant sequelae, compared with 1 to 5 percent following partial moles.

211
00:25:05,036 --> 00:25:13,074
Surprisingly, with much earlier recognition and evacuation of molar pregnancies, the risk for GTN has not dropped .

212
00:25:13,074 --> 00:25:37,086
Other GTN risk factors are older maternal age, preevacuation β-hCG levels >100,000 mIU/mL, uterine size that is large-for-gestational age, theca-lutein cysts >6 cm, and a slow decline in β-hCG levels (Berkowitz, 2009; Kang, 2012; Wolfberg, 2005).

213
00:25:37,086 --> 00:25:40,274
Gestational Trophoblastic Neoplasia.

214
00:25:40,274 --> 00:25:49,224
This group includes invasive mole, choriocarcinoma, placental site trophoblastic tumor, and epithelioid trophoblastic tumor.

215
00:25:49,224 --> 00:25:55,011
These tumors almost always develop with or after some form of recognized pregnancy.

216
00:25:55,011 --> 00:26:04,124
Half follow hydatidiform mole, a fourth follow miscarriage or tubal pregnancy, and another fourth develop after a preterm or term pregnancy .

217
00:26:04,124 --> 00:26:15,699
These four tumor types are histologically distinct but are usually diagnosed solely by persistently elevated serum β-hCG levels because tissue is infrequently available for study.

218
00:26:15,699 --> 00:26:21,436
Criteria to diagnose postmolar GTN are shown in Table 13-4.

219
00:26:21,436 --> 00:26:23,961
Table 13-4.

220
00:26:23,961 --> 00:26:32,449
Criteria for Diagnosis of Gestational Trophoblastic Neoplasia # Diagnosis, Staging, and Prognostic Scoring.

221
00:26:32,449 --> 00:26:40,936
Clinically, these placental tumors are characterized by their aggressive invasion into the myometrium and propensity to metastasize.

222
00:26:40,936 --> 00:26:47,249
The most common finding with GTN is irregular bleeding associated with uterine subinvolution.

223
00:26:47,249 --> 00:26:52,561
The bleeding may be continuous or intermittent and sometimes may be sudden and massive.

224
00:26:52,561 --> 00:26:58,561
Myometrial perforation from trophoblastic growth can cause intraperitoneal hemorrhage.

225
00:26:58,561 --> 00:27:03,049
In some women, lower genital tract metastases are evident.

226
00:27:03,049 --> 00:27:08,986
In others, only distant metastases, with no trace of uterine tumor, are found.

227
00:27:08,986 --> 00:27:15,049
Consideration for the possibility of GTN is the most important factor in its recognition.

228
00:27:15,049 --> 00:27:22,174
Unusually persistent bleeding after any type of pregnancy should prompt serum β-hCG level measurement.

229
00:27:22,174 --> 00:27:31,174
Uterine size is assessed, and careful examination seeks lower genital tract metastases, which usually are bluish vascular masses .

230
00:27:31,174 --> 00:27:36,699
Tissue biopsy of such masses is unnecessary and may cause significant bleeding.

231
00:27:36,699 --> 00:27:43,711
Once the diagnosis is verified, a baseline serum β-hCG level and hemogram are obtained.

232
00:27:43,711 --> 00:27:57,274
A search for local disease and metastases includes tests of liver and renal function, transvaginal sonography, chest CT or radiography, and brain and abdominopelvic CT scan or MR imaging.

233
00:27:57,274 --> 00:28:07,849
Less often, positron-emission tomographic (PET) scanning and cerebrospinal fluid β-hCG level determination are used to identify metastases .

234
00:28:07,849 --> 00:28:14,061
If no extrauterine disease is found, a second curettage or hysterectomy may be considered.

235
00:28:14,061 --> 00:28:22,074
After either, β-hCG levels are then measured every 2 weeks until three consecutively lie in undetectable range.

236
00:28:22,074 --> 00:28:26,124
Levels are subsequently repeated monthly for 6 months.

237
00:28:26,124 --> 00:28:41,924
In contrast, if β-hCG levels persist after curettage or hysterectomy or if initial extrauterine disease is found, patients undergo staging and chemotherapy is instituted (Koh, 2018; Osborne, 2016).

238
00:28:41,924 --> 00:28:50,861
GTN is staged clinically using the system of the International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO) (2009).

239
00:28:50,861 --> 00:29:05,011
This includes a modification of the World Health Organization (WHO) (1983) prognostic index score, with which scores of 0 to 4 are given for each of the categories shown in Table 13-5.

240
00:29:05,011 --> 00:29:14,736
Women with WHO scores of 0 to 6 are considered to have lowrisk disease, whereas those with scores ≥7 are considered in the high-risk group.

241
00:29:14,736 --> 00:29:17,361
TABLE 13-5.

242
00:29:17,361 --> 00:29:29,349
International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO) Staging and Diagnostic Scoring System for Gestational Trophoblastic Neoplasia # Histological Classification.

243
00:29:29,349 --> 00:29:33,524
Clinical staging is assigned without regard to histological

244
00:29:33,676 --> 00:29:36,363
findings, even if available.

245
00:29:36,313 --> 00:29:40,363
Still, distinct histological types are recognized.

246
00:29:40,363 --> 00:29:48,788
Invasive Mole These are a common trophoblastic neoplasm, and almost all invasive moles arise from partial or complete moles.

247
00:29:48,788 --> 00:29:58,013
Previously known as chorioadenoma destruens, invasive mole is characterized by extensive tissue invasion by trophoblast and whole villi.

248
00:29:58,013 --> 00:30:06,913
Trophoblastic cells penetrate deep into the myometrium and sometimes involve the peritoneum, adjacent parametrium, or vaginal vault.

249
00:30:06,913 --> 00:30:12,088
Although locally aggressive, invasive moles are less prone to metastasize.

250
00:30:12,088 --> 00:30:23,251
Gestational Choriocarcinoma This is the most common trophoblastic neoplasm to follow a term pregnancy or a miscarriage, and only a fourth of cases follow a molar gestation .

251
00:30:23,251 --> 00:30:32,401
Choriocarcinoma is composed of cells reminiscent of early cytotrophoblast and syncytiotrophoblast, however, it contains no villi.

252
00:30:32,401 --> 00:30:39,113
This rapidly growing tumor invades both myometrium and blood vessels to create hemorrhage and necrosis.

253
00:30:39,113 --> 00:30:46,301
Myometrial tumor may spread outward and become visible on the uterine surface as dark, irregular nodules.

254
00:30:46,301 --> 00:30:50,663
Metastases often develop early and are generally blood-borne.

255
00:30:50,663 --> 00:30:59,426
The most common sites are the lungs and vagina, but tumor may travel to the vulva, kidneys, liver, brain, ovaries, and bowel.

256
00:30:59,426 --> 00:31:04,888
With choriocarcinomas, ovarian theca-lutein cysts commonly coexist.

257
00:31:04,888 --> 00:31:12,488
Placental Site Trophoblastic Tumor This uncommon tumor arises from intermediate trophoblasts at the placental site.

258
00:31:12,488 --> 00:31:18,926
These tumors have associated serum β-hCG levels that may be only modestly elevated .

259
00:31:18,926 --> 00:31:28,001
However, they produce variant forms of hCG, and identifying a high proportion of free β-hCG favors this diagnosis .

260
00:31:28,001 --> 00:31:37,226
Treatment of placental site trophoblastic tumor by hysterectomy is preferred because these locally invasive tumors are usually resistant to chemotherapy.

261
00:31:37,226 --> 00:31:47,251
For higher-risk stage I and for later stages, adjuvant multidrug chemotherapy also is given (Koh, 2018; Schmid, 2009).

262
00:31:47,251 --> 00:31:54,151
Epithelioid Trophoblastic Tumor This rare tumor develops from chorionic-type intermediate trophoblast.

263
00:31:54,151 --> 00:32:00,338
The uterus is mainly involved, and bleeding but low hCG levels are typical findings .

264
00:32:00,338 --> 00:32:06,488
Primary treatment is hysterectomy because this tumor is relatively resistant to chemotherapy.

265
00:32:06,488 --> 00:32:15,576
Metastatic disease is common, and combination chemotherapy is employed (Frijstein, 2019; Koh, 2018).

266
00:32:15,576 --> 00:32:17,651
# Treatment.

267
00:32:17,651 --> 00:32:26,013
Women with GTN are best managed by oncologists, and some evidence supports treatment in centers specializing in GTN .

268
00:32:26,013 --> 00:32:29,863
Chemotherapy alone is usually the primary treatment.

269
00:32:29,863 --> 00:32:46,276
In some GTN cases without extrauterine disease, a second uterine evacuation may be an adjuvant therapeutic option to avoid or minimize chemotherapy (Hemida, 2019; Koh, 2018; Pezeshki, 2004).

270
00:32:46,276 --> 00:32:55,013
In other cases, suction curettage may infrequently be needed to resolve bleeding or remove a substantial amount of retained molar tissue.

271
00:32:55,013 --> 00:33:04,313
In specific cases, hysterectomy may be primary or adjuvant treatment (Bolze, 2018; Eysbouts, 2017b).

272
00:33:04,313 --> 00:33:15,613
Single-agent chemotherapy protocols are usually sufficient for nonmetastatic or low-risk metastatic neoplasia (Lawrie, 2016; Koh, 2018).

273
00:33:15,613 --> 00:33:30,601
In their review of 108 women with low-risk disease, Abrão and colleagues (2008) reported that monotherapy protocols with either methotrexate or actinomycin D were equally effective compared with a regimen containing both.

274
00:33:30,601 --> 00:33:38,801
In general, methotrexate is less toxic than actinomycin D (Chan, 2006; Seckl, 2010).

275
00:33:38,801 --> 00:33:44,013
Regimens are repeated until serum β-hCG levels are undetectable.

276
00:33:44,013 --> 00:33:51,351
Combination chemotherapy is given for high-risk disease, and reported cure rates approximate 90 percent .

277
00:33:51,351 --> 00:33:54,101
Several regimens are successful.

278
00:33:54,101 --> 00:34:03,476
One is EMA-CO, which includes etoposide, methotrexate, actinomycin D, cyclophosphamide, and vincristine (Oncovin).

279
00:34:03,476 --> 00:34:09,176
In selected cases, adjuvant surgical and radiotherapy also may be employed .

280
00:34:09,176 --> 00:34:26,701
Despite chemotherapy successes in general, frequent causes of death include hemorrhage from metastatic sites, respiratory failure, sepsis, and multiorgan failure due to widespread chemoresistant disease (Lybol, 2012; Neubauer, 2015).

281
00:34:26,701 --> 00:34:34,251
With either low- or high-risk disease, once serum β-hCG levels are undetectable, serosurveillance is

282
00:34:34,420 --> 00:34:36,682
continued for 1 year.

283
00:34:36,632 --> 00:34:49,320
During this time, effective contraception is crucial to avoid teratogenic effects of chemotherapy to the fetus and to mitigate confusion from rising β-hCG levels caused by superimposed pregnancy.

284
00:34:49,320 --> 00:34:58,195
A few women during surveillance, despite no evidence of metastases, will be found to have very low β-hCG levels that plateau.

285
00:34:58,195 --> 00:35:04,557
This phenomenon is called quiescent hCG and presumably stems from dormant trophoblast.

286
00:35:04,557 --> 00:35:12,695
Close observation without therapy is recommended, but 20 percent will eventually have recurrent active and progressive GTN .

287
00:35:12,695 --> 00:35:15,057
Subsequent Pregnancy.

288
00:35:15,057 --> 00:35:28,782
Women with prior hydatidiform mole generally do not have impaired fertility, and their pregnancy outcomes are usually normal (Joneborg, 2014; Matsui, 2001; Sebire, 2003).

289
00:35:28,782 --> 00:35:36,895
One concern is the 1-percent risk for developing trophoblastic disease in a subsequent pregnancy (p. 237).

290
00:35:36,895 --> 00:35:43,070
Sonographic evaluation is recommended in early pregnancy, and subsequently as needed.

291
00:35:43,070 --> 00:35:49,907
Women with GTN who have successfully completed chemotherapy are advised to delay pregnancy for 1 year.

292
00:35:49,907 --> 00:35:53,482
Most relapses develop within this time period .

293
00:35:53,482 --> 00:35:58,095
Also, methotrexate may persist in human tissues for months .

294
00:35:58,095 --> 00:36:06,520
Despite this, women who become pregnant within 1 year postchemotherapy for GTN can be reassured of a likely favorable outcome .

295
00:36:06,520 --> 00:36:14,470
Risk of miscarriage or GTN relapse is not increased compared with women conceiving after the suggested 1-year surveillance .

296
00:36:14,470 --> 00:36:25,232
However, these gravidas are advised that the diagnosis of a tumor relapse may be delayed during pregnancy (Blagden, 2002; Tuncer, 1999a).

297
00:36:25,232 --> 00:36:37,645
In general, fertility and pregnancy outcomes following GTN treatment are typically normal, and congenital anomaly rates are not increased (Berkowitz, 2000; Tse, 2012).

298
00:36:37,645 --> 00:36:45,157
One exception is a higher stillbirth rate of 1.5 percent compared with a background rate of 0.8 percent .

299
00:36:45,157 --> 00:36:56,507
For those with prior hydatidiform mole or GTN treatment, the placenta or products of conception in a subsequent pregnancy are sent for pathological evaluation at delivery.

300
00:36:56,507 --> 00:37:07,607
A serum β-hCG level also is measured 6 weeks postpartum (Lurain, 2010; Royal College of Obstetricians and Gynaecologists, 2010).

301
00:37:07,607 --> 00:37:14,695
This assay may be less valuable for those solely with a prior mole compared with those previously treated for GTN .

